  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Lehualide A may interact with Acyl-CoA hydrolase through multiple indirect pathways. The drug is predicted to target Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits, which are subsequently targeted by (2R,3R)-3-hydroxy-5-oxooxolane-2,3-dicarboxylic acid and Methyl 14-methylhexadecanoate. Both of these compounds are known to inhibit Acyl-CoA hydrolase, indicating a potential mechanism of action involving the inhibition of this enzyme. Additionally, Lehualide A is also predicted to interact with PITSLRE serine/threonine-protein kinase CDC2L2, which is targeted by a different compound that also inhibits Acyl-CoA hydrolase. This suggests that Lehualide A may exert its effects through multiple distinct pathways, potentially involving different molecular targets. The evidence is consistent across different scoring metrics, indicating a plausible relationship between Lehualide A and Acyl-CoA hydrolase, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
